# Build and interpreter artifacts
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/

# Disposable caches and logs
cache/
*.log
*.sqlite3-journal
# Hand-redirected gate output. *.log above covers the well-named case; these are
# the ad-hoc names that leaked into the tree root.
/.g?.txt
gate*.txt

# Fetched external material (writeups and raw responses). It is untrusted, may
# hold flags, credentials or other people's data, and is reproducible by
# re-running the pinned pipeline; never commit it.
knowledge/raw/

# Per-run and per-challenge working state; regenerated, and can hold target data
**/runs/
**/state.json
**/baseline.json
!test/baseline.json

# NOT build artifacts: build/ holds the four generators that PRODUCE
# knowledge/bug-classes.json, the bug-class skills, skills/INDEX.md and
# skills/registry.json. Ignoring it kept only their output in history, and
# test/regression.py reads build/make_class_skills.py, so a fresh clone could
# not pass its own gate. Track the generators; ignore nothing here.

# Transient agent worktrees. Each is a full copy of the tree (636 MB when this
# was written) and is deleted when its agent finishes.
.claude/worktrees/

# Fetched writeup corpus. Same rule as knowledge/raw/ above: external material
# that holds other people's flags — ctf_writeups.csv carries ten of them — and
# is reproducible by re-running the collection. Keep it locally, never commit it.
Writeup/

# Large vendored binaries: keep them out of history
tools/ghidra-dist/
*.zip
*.tar.gz
*.iso
*.vmdk

# Challenge inputs and artifacts. They are large, often redistributable only by
# the event, and can contain flags. Track one deliberately with
#   git add -f "challenges/<name>"
# scripts/repo_init.sh does exactly that for the Weather App regression fixture.
challenges/

# Per-event working directories hold the same thing challenges/ does -- handout
# source, organizer material, and real flags -- plus generated runtime
# credentials. CSCV2026/ carries an event writeup with a live flag and
# diemthi/runtime/jsreport-pass. tools/crypto/selftest.py reads
# CSCV2026/Crypto/solve/output_typhon.txt for its real-data case and SKIPS when
# it is absent, which is why ignoring this costs a clone nothing but the skip.
CSCV2026/
# Media never belongs in history, even inside a force-added challenge
*.gif
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.png
*.mp4
*.pcap
*.raw
*.dmp

# Secrets: never commit, not even to a private repository
/tmp/
.env
*.token
*.secret
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*

# Transient review digest produced by hand for the field-note queue; regenerate, never commit.
REVIEW_QUEUE.md
